### ETH-20: sepolia_balance.py sends a descriptive User-Agent

- Public Sepolia RPC endpoints (publicnode, drpc, 1rpc, rpc.sepolia.org,
  rpc2.sepolia.org, blastapi) answer HTTP 403 to urllib's default
  "Python-urllib/3.x" agent, so the script's compiled-in default endpoint
  failed on every node. The request now sends "aitbc-sepolia-balance/1.0",
  which publicnode accepts (verified 200 vs 403); a unit test pins the
  non-default agent against a loopback stub.

### MSG-8g — DM read falls back across stored key eras; MCP plaintext wording corrected

- `e6bdab745` fix(messaging): `aitbc.dm_client` gains `DmDecryptError` (a `ValueError` subclass raised only for the SealedBox `CryptoError` case, same message as before) and `open_dm_with_eras()`, which tries the stored secret list newest to oldest and returns the first body that opens. Only a decrypt failure advances to the next era; a malformed ciphertext, non-UTF-8 payload or `aitbc-dm-v1` header mismatch raises on the spot — a tampered or misrouted message is never retried against older keys. When no era opens, one `ValueError` names only the tried count — no key, era or ciphertext material. `dm read` (CLI, via `_dm_key_eras`) and `MessagingClient.dm_read` (SDK) both walk the wallet's full `dm-keys` era list through the shared helper, so a DM sealed to a superseded `dm_key` still opens after `dm keygen --rotate` (MSG-VERIFY-1 G2). An explicit SDK `secret_key=` keeps its single-key meaning; corrupt stores still raise `DmStoreCorruptError` untouched; the empty-store and single-era paths are unchanged.
- Same commit, docs: the "MCP layer never sees plaintext" claim was wrong — the generated `aitbc_messaging_dm_*` tools shell out to `aitbc` on the resolved host (local subprocess or `ssh` via `_run_remote`) and return its stdout in the tool result, so DM plaintext transits the MCP server process and reaches a remote MCP client; key material is still read only on the host the CLI runs on. `docs/design/msg8-direct-messages.md` §5 and `docs/features/agent-messaging-paths.md` now say so (G4), and the design's sender-copy section records that Phase 1 implements no sender copy at all (recommendation (a) deferred — G3).
- Same commit, tests: `TestOpenDmWithEras` kernel coverage in `apps/blockchain-node/tests/test_messaging_dm.py`; CLI-era coverage (`TestDmReadEras`) in `cli/tests/test_messaging_dm.py`; SDK-era coverage in `packages/py/aitbc-sdk/tests/test_messaging_dm.py` — rotation round trip on both read paths, tried-count reporting without key/ciphertext material, header-failure no-fall-through (call-count spy), tampered-ciphertext-across-all-eras, single/empty/corrupt store parity and the explicit-`secret_key` single-attempt path.

### ETH-20b — `aitbc wallet eth-balance`: typed read-only Sepolia balance/nonce command + generated MCP tool

- New command `aitbc wallet eth-balance` prints the sealed-state (`latest` block tag) `eth_getBalance` and `eth_getTransactionCount` for one address: `--address` (EIP-55 validated, mixed-case checksum failures refuse; zero address and self-reads are fine — those gates were send-safety only) or `--wallet <name|path>` (plaintext `address` field only — no decryption, no password resolution, no key access). Exactly one selector is required. The endpoint resolves `--eth-rpc-url` > `ETH_RPC_URL` env > CLI config and the endpoint's own `eth_chainId` must answer 11155111. Output: `{address, balance_wei, balance_eth, nonce, rpc}` with the masked display URL (provider keys in the path never reach error text).
- Shared helpers: `_send_wallet`/`_wallet_address_only`/`_check_rpc_chain` from `eth_send.py` and `_resolve_wallet_path` from `bridge.py` moved into `commands/wallet/eth_common.py` (public names) — no private cross-module imports; `eth-send` and `bridge` behaviour unchanged.
- User-Agent: verified live from node2 that the compiled-in publicnode endpoint accepts httpx's default `python-httpx` agent (HTTP 200, valid `eth_chainId`) — no explicit UA needed in `rpc_call` (unlike the urllib path ETH-20 fixed).
- MCP: `aitbc_wallet_eth_balance` generated into `aitbc_mcp_cli_tools_generated_wallet.py`, classified read-only (`read_only_hint=True`, `_aitbc_cli_read_tool`, no dry_run/confirm) via a new `FORCE_READ_ONLY_PATHS` entry in the generator.
- Tests: new `cli/tests/test_wallet_eth_balance.py` (15 cases: happy paths, address/checksum/selector refusals, unresolved RPC, RPC error, wallet-file read never touching `private_key`, non-Sepolia refusal, masked-URL error text); `test_wallet_eth_send.py` fixture extended for the helper move. `cli/README.md` and `cli/CLI_USAGE_GUIDE.md` list the new subcommand.


### CLI: `market list`/`providers` fall back to the local market service when the hub name is unreachable

- `ffa031662` fix(cli): on the hub itself `https://<hub>` resolves to the TLS edge, which the node cannot hairpin back to, so every hub-facing read failed with "connection refused". New `_hub_base_url` keeps an explicit scheme in `HUB_DISCOVERY_URL`, maps localhost to http, and only upgrades a bare host to https (TLS termination lives on the fronting proxy); `_candidate_bases` retries the same reads against `market_service_url` (`http://127.0.0.1:8102`). `aitbc market list` and `aitbc market providers` now work on the hub and degrade gracefully on any node whose hub edge is down. `cancel`, `offer`, `offer-list` and `offer-disable` share `_hub_base_url`, so an explicit `http(s)://` value in `HUB_DISCOVERY_URL` is honoured everywhere.
- Same commit: the `/rpc/transactions` reads in `market list`'s chain fallback switched to `get_json()` — the endpoint returns a bare JSON array that `get()` rejects, which previously turned the local-RPC fallback into a TypeError abort ("returned a JSON array, not an object").
- Tests: `tests/cli/test_market_offers.py` gains `TestHubBaseUrl` (scheme matrix) and `TestMarketListFallback` (hub refused → local market service serves the list; bare-array RPC response accepted).

### Config/CLI: hub-ward URLs resolve locally on the hub; explicit schemes honored

`aitbc.config.hub` detects when the node *is* the hub (`BLOCKCHAIN_MODE=hub`,
or `NODE_ID`/`AITBC_HOSTNAME`/`P2P_NODE_ID` matching the discovery host) and
resolves the hub origin to `HUB_LOCAL_URL` or local nginx `http://127.0.0.1`
— the public name cannot hairpin back there. `hub_origin()`/`hub_service_url()`
now honor explicit `http://` schemes instead of always upgrading to https;
new `normalize_hub_base()`/`is_hub_self()` helpers back every naive call site
(market offers/jobs/escrow/ratings/status/match, network, ipfs, dashboard,
transactions, gpu-onchain, island join). `CLIConfig.hub_blockchain_rpc_url`
derives via `hub_service_url("rpc")` and covers `HUB_P2P_HOST`-only nodes.
Fixes hub-local `market list`/`providers`, `escrow`, `status`, `match` and the
fleet-wide-dead `gpu-onchain`/`island join` hub fallbacks; followers unchanged.

### CLI: `exchange-island`/`market`/`wallet` read the array-shaped `/rpc/transactions` via `get_json`

The endpoint returns a bare JSON array; `AITBCHTTPClient.get()` rejects
non-object bodies, so `exchange-island orderbook`/`orders`/`rates`,
`market` offer resolution's chain fallback, and `wallet transactions`
all failed fleet-wide before reaching their (already shape-tolerant)
normalizers. Ops fix on hub: `island_credentials.json` `rpc_endpoint`
now `http://127.0.0.1:8202/rpc` — the previous `https://hub.aitbc.bubuit.net`
value hairpin-failed from inside the hub (fulfils the ops item CLI-3
deferred).

### CLI: aitbc pool-hub on followers used http://{hub}/pool-hub — plain HTTP against a TLS-only edge. Resolved via normalize_hub_base() → https on followers, http://127.0.0.1 on hub. The nginx /pool-hub/ mount already existed; only the scheme was wrong. Also: test_find_service_by_type_and_name now sets API_KEY_STORAGE_PATH → tmp so importing the services router no longer requires /var/lib/aitbc.
